3.11 From certainty to uncertainty
The previous sections show that while the rules of logic are important for the general structure of scientific argument (the most basic of them being that a thing cannot be TRUE and FALSE at the same time), they are clearly not sufficient for explaining, or modelling, or prescribing the scientific argument. This does not mean that a scientific argument is extra-logical or illogical; it means that we need an expanded, non-monotonic logic that can deal with uncertainty.
Collecting the requirements accumulated over this chapter, the expanded logic must:

reduce to classical logic when uncertainty is reduced to certainty;
be inductive — able to generalise from particular data to general conclusions — and therefore non-monotonic: new data must be able to revise a conclusion;
quantify uncertainty, giving it numerical values, since general conclusions drawn from particular data cannot be certain (uncertainty can never beget certainty);
license the weak syllogisms: when A -&gt; B holds and B is observed, the calculus must say by how much A gains in plausibility;
protect its user from sure loss, in the contract sense of this chapter, when commitments come in degrees.

This expanded logic is probability theory, and it quantifies uncertainty as probabilities — real numbers between 0 and 1 that conform to a small set of rules. Chapter 6 and Chapter 8 derive those rules twice, by two independent routes: from counting (Pascal and Fermat, formalised by Kolmogorov, in Chapter 6), and from desiderata on the robot’s degrees of plausibility (Cox and Jaynes, in Chapter 8); the Dutch book argument, also in Chapter 8, shows that the same rules are forced by the no-sure-loss requirement. That such different starting points yield one calculus is the main reason for taking that calculus seriously as the logic of science.
One direction of expansion is worth marking now, precisely because the list above does not ask for it. Probability theory expands logic upwards, replacing two truth values with a continuum of degrees; it does not expand it sideways, into the quantifiers and relations of predicate logic. The propositional skeleton noted at the opening of this chapter therefore remains a skeleton: the robot of Chapter 8 grades propositions without looking inside them, and the relational structure that a causal claim requires — which unit, related to which other unit, under which intervention — has to be supplied by a second formalism (Part III). Chapter 8 returns to this as the fourth limit of the robot.
Part II builds the expanded logic. Before it does, the next two chapters take up the book’s other thread, the one this chapter could only mark as missing: Chapter 4 asks what a causal claim means and why the randomised experiment is the cleanest way to establish one, and Chapter 5 surveys the theories of causation and says which the book adopts. The counterfactual “if” that this chapter showed to be non-truth-functional is where they begin.

\(P(A) = 0\) — A is certainly false;
\(P(A) = 0.5\) — A and not-A are equally plausible;
\(P(A) = 1\) — A is certainly true;

we want the causal effect of \(x_1\) on y, and \(x_2\) is a confounder we must adjust for – the reason that dominates Part III of this book, and the one where a DAG decides which covariates belong in the model (Chapter 12);
we want to predict y as accurately as possible, in which case any predictor that carries information is welcome, whether or not it causes anything (Chapter 27). This reason is incompatible with the first one, as the variables whose inclusion in the model destroys our causal inference are often useful for predicting y;
\(x_2\) is a competing cause of y that we are not interested in but which, once accounted for, shrinks \(\sigma\) and so sharpens the estimate of the effect we do care about. This reason is perfectly compatible with the first one.








There is no reason to think that if we compare the models \(\mu_i = \alpha + \beta x_{i1}\) and \(\mu_i = \alpha + \beta_1 x_{i1} + \beta_2 x_{i2}\), then \(\beta\) and \(\beta_1\) will be similar, or even have the same sign. Which of the two is the quantity you want depends on the role \(x_2\) plays in the causal graph (Chapter 12), and the possibilities do not all point the same way:

if \(x_2\) is a mediator on the path from \(x_1\) to y, then \(\beta\) estimates the total effect and \(\beta_1\) the direct effect (given no confounding, no interaction, and no exposure-induced confounding of the mediator – Chapter 16);
if \(x_2\) is a confounder of \(x_1\) and y, the labels swap: the adjusted \(\beta_1\) is the estimate of the total effect, and the unadjusted \(\beta\) is biased;
if \(x_2\) is a collider on a path between \(x_1\) and y, adjusting for it introduces bias, and neither coefficient estimates an effect;
if \(x_2\) is simply another cause of y, unrelated to \(x_1\), both estimate the same total effect, and the adjusted one is more precise because \(\sigma\) is smaller.

The regression arithmetic is identical in all four cases. Only the DAG distinguishes them. This is why the adjustment set is settled before the fit and cannot be recovered from it, a point Chapter 23 returns to with a numerical demonstration.
If you have several predictors in your regression, then for easier comparison of the betas it makes sense to standardise them. A standardised beta is the change in mean y per one sd of that predictor as it varies in this sample, so the comparison is a comparison of how much each predictor contributes to the observed variation in y here – not of how large its causal effect is. A predictor that was measured over a narrow range in this study will have a small standardised beta even if its effect per unit is large.
If you have only continuous predictors, use x_st = (x - mean(x))/sd(x). Then all your predictors have mean = 0 and sd = 1, the intercept is the value of y when all predictors are at their mean values (which is now 0), and each slope gives the change in mean y if the relevant predictor changes by one standard deviation, all other predictors remaining unchanged.
If you have both continuous and binary predictors, you get approximate comparability of the betas with x_st2 = (x - mean(x))/(2*sd(x)), which rescales a continuous predictor to a two-sd change rather than a one-sd change. The reason for the factor of 2 is comparability with an untransformed 0/1 indicator: a binary variable with p = 0.5 has an sd of 0.5, so switching it from 0 to 1 is already a two-sd change. Dividing the continuous predictors by twice their sd therefore puts both kinds on the same footing.4 As before, all other X-s are held constant and the intercept refers to the mean values of all of them.
Centring a continuous predictor changes what the intercept means and what a prior on it asserts. The cohort work below uses age in decades centred at 62, so that the intercept refers to a 62-year-old and the age coefficient, and its prior, are per decade. Once a predictor enters a product term the centring is no longer optional, because on the raw scale the product is nearly collinear with its parents and the sampler struggles (Chapter 24).
